erties of grid-like representations, especially mathematical relationships with reinforcement learning and word embedding methods. Our model deepens theoretical interpretations of grid-like representations and extends the range of application to general decision making and conceptual processing.\nVector-based analogical inference by our model is a same function as word embedding methods in NLP (Mikolov et al., 2013a;b; Pennington et al., 2014; Levy & Goldberg, 2014). However, a unique feature of DSI representation vectors is that each dimension of vectors corresponds to a specific concept like concept cells in the human brain (Quiroga, 2012; Reber et al., 2019), and each word is represented by a combination of concepts. Furthermore, previous word embedding methods have been developed only for NLP, thus relationships with other functions such as reinforcement learning and spatial navigation have not been extensively explored. It was recently shown that transformer-based models (Vaswani et al., 2017; Brown et al., 2020), which are currently state-ofthe-art models in NLP, generate grid-like representations when those models are applied to spatial learning (Whittington et al., 2022). Similarly to our model, this finding implies the relationship between spatial and linguistic processing in the brain. However, the emergence of concept-specific representations and clear theoretical interpretability are still unique features of our model."
