      {
        "heading": "METHOD SIMILARITY ANALOGY",
        "text": "gest that spatial and conceptual processing can be theoretically unified into a single vector-based computational principle, and the spatial processing system in the brain may rely on that principle.\nIn contrast with successor representation which is tightly related to value functions in conventional reinforcement learning, our DSI model approximates value functions in linear reinforcement learning. An important feature of linear reinforcement learning is that value functions are directly determined from representations learned under a default policy, while rigorous calculation of conventional value functions requires representations under an optimal policy. It would be intriguing to clarify the relationship between a choice of default policy and the bias of representations and navigation functions (Piray & Daw, 2021), which may correspond to the experimentally observed distortion of grid cells (Boccara et al., 2019). Furthermore, how we can extend such theoretical concepts of reinforcement learning into conceptual processing remains to be addressed. Such extension may open the way to understand conceptual processing from the theory of decision making.\nIn the section 5.2, we showed that our DSI model creates concept-specific representations from text data. To the best of our knowledge, such property has not been reported in any previous word embedding methods. However, in the field of deep generative models, \u201cdisentanglement\u201d of latent representations enables the formation of visual feature-specific representations (Higgins et al., 2017). We speculate that our model performs something similar to such methods, that is, disentanglement of word representations. As disentangled visual representations explain neural activities in higher-order visual cortex (Higgins et al., 2021), we may similarly interpret concept cells in the human medial temporal lobe as disentangled conceptual representations. Furthermore, grid cells may also emerge as disentangled spatial representations for 2-D structures.\nOur model implies the relationship between word embedding methods and conceptual representations in the brain. Previously, it was shown that word representation vectors obtained by Skip-gram support high-performance decoding of semantic information from the brain (Nishida & Nishimoto, 2018), which experimentally supports this idea. However, with regard to a biologically plausible linguistic representation model, recent studies have shown that representations in transformer-based models (Vaswani et al., 2017) such as GPT (Brown et al., 2020) achieve great performance in linear fitting to neural recording (Goldstein et al., 2022; Schrimpf et al., 2021). A major difference between our DSI model and transformer-based models is that DSI representations are basically fixed (static embedding) whereas transformer-based models flexibly create context-dependent representations (dynamic embedding). Conceptual interpretation obviously depends on the context, thus activities of concept cells are context-dependent (Bausch et al., 2021). Therefore, our DSI model should be extended to process context-dependence, hopefully by combination with other models that enable learning of latent cognitive states (Uria et al., 2020; George et al., 2021; Whittington et al., 2020; 2022). This extension also leads to flexible decision making and navigation.\nAnother direction of future research is learning DSI representations from low-level sensory inputs, like spatial learning from visual and auditory inputs in previous models (Banino et al., 2018; Taniguchi et al., 2018; Uria et al., 2020). As for the human brain, infants probably form primitive spatial and conceptual representations from sensory signals, and later linguistic inputs enrich those representations. We speculate that real-world sensory data also contain the information of the conceptual space, for which DSI can be extended to learn those structures. Such model would clarify the